      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "A gladiator escaped his school and raised an army that shook Rome."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.034.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "The breakout",
      "body": "In 73 BCE Spartacus, a gladiator from Thrace, escaped from a gladiator school at Capua with a band of fellow fighters. Enslaved people from Italy’s great estates joined him. His army grew to tens of thousands and defeated several Roman forces."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.034.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "A brutal end",
      "body": "In 71 BCE the wealthy general Marcus Crassus crushed the revolt. Some 6,000 captured rebels were crucified along the Appian Way as a warning. Spartacus himself became a lasting symbol of resistance to oppression."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.034.c4",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "connect",
      "headline": "Why Rome was afraid",
      "body": "The great estates that the Gracchi had tried to break up were worked by enslaved people. That is why a slave army frightened wealthy Romans so much: their fortunes depended on the very people now in revolt."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.034.c5",
